## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design real-time blind source separation systems for mobile platforms with dynamic geometry and environmental noise challenges, emphasizing adaptive signal processing, cross-bin coherence, and multimodal self-supervised learning approaches.

## Key Concepts

- Geometry-aware whitening with adaptive tracking
- Online ICA versus joint diagonalization strategies
- Dynamic demixer scaling and computational cadence
- Cross-bin permutation alignment using envelope analysis
- Video-driven self-supervised permutation stabilization

## Code Example

```javascript
// Geometry-aware online whitening with forgetting factor
function geometryAwareWhitening(micSignals, forgettingFactor = 0.95) {
  const covariance = updateCovariance(micSignals, forgettingFactor);
  const whiteningMatrix = computeWhiteningMatrix(covariance);
  return applyWhitening(micSignals, whiteningMatrix);
}

// Adaptive switching between per-bin ICA and joint diagonalization
function selectICAStrategy(signalQuality, frequencyBins) {
  if (signalQuality > THRESHOLD_HIGH) {
    return 'jointDiagonalization';
  } else if (signalQuality > THRESHOLD_LOW) {
    return 'perBinICA';
  }
  return 'hybridApproach';
}

// Cross-bin permutation alignment using envelope correlation
function alignPermutations(separatedSources, sampleRate) {
  const envelopes = computeEnvelopes(separatedSources);
  const correlationMatrix = computeEnvelopeCorrelation(envelopes);
  return resolvePermutationAmbiguity(correlationMatrix);
}

// Self-supervised loss using synchronized video
function videoGuidedLoss(audioSources, videoFeatures) {
  const audioVisualCorrelation = computeCrossModalCorrelation(audioSources, videoFeatures);
  return -audioVisualCorrelation.mean(); // Minimize negative correlation
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to combine multi-modal signals with ICA in real time—a practical challenge in modern audio-visual setups used by leading tech companies for conferencing, broadcasting, and assistive listening devices.

## Key Concepts
- Audio-visual fusion for permutation resolution in frequency-domain ICA
- Online ICA with dynamic source count adaptation (2–3 speakers)
- Latency budgets and robustness to video failures
- Feature design for lip cues under variable lighting conditions

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: weight update between audio-ICA demixer and video cues
function updateDemixer(audioState, videoState) {
  const wVideo = computeVideoBias(videoState); // robust lip-motion features
  const wAudio = 1 - wVideo;

  const audioUpdate = computeAudioICUpdate(audioState);
  const videoUpdate = computeVideoPermutationBias(videoState);

  return wAudio * audioUpdate + wVideo * videoUpdate;
}

function computeVideoBias(videoState) {
  const lightingCondition = assessLighting(videoState.features);
  const faceDetectionConfidence = videoState.detectionScore;
  return lightingCondition * faceDetectionConfidence;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design a robust, semi-supervised blind source separation (BSS) system under dynamic conditions, with automatic configuration of source count and permutation handling.

## Key Concepts
- Semi-supervised bootstrap for source counting
- Online convolutive ICA with forgetting-factor whitening
- Dynamic demixer sizing and cross-bin permutation alignment
- Real-time constraints and on-device evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
function onlineICAFrame(frame, state) {
  // Pseudo implementation
  const whitened = applyForgettingFactorWhitening(frame, state);
  const demixed = applyDemixer(whitened, state.demixer);
  const sourceCount = detectActiveSources(demixed, state);
  const resizedDemixer = resizeDemixer(state.demixer, sourceCount);
  const aligned = crossBinAlignment(demixed);
  state = updateState(state, aligned, resizedDemixer);
  return { output: aligned, state };
}
```

## Key Implementation Details

### Whitening
The forgetting-factor whitening approach maintains an exponential moving average of the input covariance matrix. This enables adaptation to non-stationary noise while stabilizing the demixer convergence. The forgetting factor typically ranges from 0.95 to 0.99 depending on the speed of environmental changes.

### Dynamic Demixer Sizing
The demixer dynamically adjusts between 2–4 sources based on real-time activity detection. This is achieved through eigenvalue analysis of the demixing matrix or energy-based source presence scoring. When speaker movement or new sources are detected, the demixer expands or contracts accordingly.

### Cross-Bin Alignment
Permutation ambiguity is addressed through frequency-bin consistency enforcement. By comparing the time-frequency structures across bins and using the labeled reference window as an anchor, we can reliably track source identity across all frequencies.

### Update Cadence
The system employs a hierarchical update strategy: fast updates (every 10–50ms) for tracking moving speakers and slow updates (every 500ms–2s) for structural adaptation. This balances responsiveness with stability.

### On-Device Evaluation Plan
Ground-truth evaluation uses pre-recorded multi-speaker sessions with known source counts and identities. Metrics include SI-SNR improvement, SDR, and PESQ scores, measured against isolated source references captured during calibration.

